<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Menashi–Kunashir rebellion</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1789</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Menashi–Kunashir rebellion was an uprising of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ainu">Ainu</a> of Kunashir Island and the Menashi district of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hokkaido">Hokkaido</a> in 1789. Provoked by harsh treatment from Japanese merchants running fisheries under the Matsumae domain, the Ainu killed about seventy Japanese. Matsumae forces suppressed it and executed thirty-seven Ainu, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a> then took a closer interest in Ezo.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/menashi-kunashir-rebellion.jpg" alt="Menashi–Kunashir rebellion" width="320" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kakizaki Hakyo (1764-1826) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ishuretsuzo_(Ikotoi)_by_Kakizaki_Hakyo_(MBAA_Besancon).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
